    "overview": {
      "format": "markdown",
      "body": "## Overview\n\nThis self-study lesson introduces heteroscedasticity, a common issue in applied economic regression analysis. You will progress from understanding residual variance, through interpreting residual plots and economic examples, to evaluating practical detection and remedy approaches used by economists and researchers."
    },
    "learning_purpose": {
      "format": "markdown",
      "body": "## Learning Purpose\n\nEconomists frequently rely on regression models to analyse relationships and inform decisions. This lesson develops the ability to recognise when the variability of residuals changes across observations, understand why this matters for statistical inference, and evaluate common practical responses. The goal is not advanced mathematics but confident interpretation of evidence and informed judgement about regression results."
    },
    "knowledge_summary": {
      "format": "markdown",
      "body": "## Knowledge Summary\n\nA regression residual is the difference between an observed value and the value predicted by a regression model. Residuals are expected in regression analysis, but their behaviour provides important diagnostic information.\n\nThe key distinction in this lesson is between homoscedasticity and heteroscedasticity. Homoscedasticity exists when residuals have a similar spread across observations. Heteroscedasticity exists when residual variance changes systematically. The issue is therefore not whether residuals are present, but whether their variability remains approximately constant.\n\nEconomic data frequently exhibit heteroscedasticity because variation in behaviour often increases as income, wealth, expenditure, or firm size increases. For example, higher-income households may display much greater diversity in spending behaviour than lower-income households, producing larger prediction errors at higher income levels.\n\nResidual plots provide an intuitive way to investigate variance patterns. A residual plot with a consistent vertical spread suggests homoscedasticity. Patterns such as widening funnels, narrowing funnels, or distinct changes in spread provide evidence consistent with heteroscedasticity. Interpretation should focus on systematic patterns in variability rather than isolated observations.\n\nA worked residual analysis follows a common reasoning process: observe residual behaviour, compare spread across observations, identify whether variance changes, and then decide whether the evidence is consistent with heteroscedasticity. This evidence-based reasoning is central to applied economic analysis.\n\nHeteroscedasticity is important because it can affect standard errors and therefore the reliability of statistical inference. Although regression coefficients may still be estimated, confidence intervals and hypothesis tests can become less dependable when constant variance assumptions are violated.\n\nEconomists therefore use diagnostic approaches and remedies. Residual plots provide visual evidence, while the Breusch–Pagan test offers a formal statistical approach to investigating whether residual variance is related to explanatory variables. Common responses include robust standard errors, which improve the reliability of inference, and variable transformations, such as logarithms, which may reduce differences in variability. Improved model specification may also reduce problematic variance patterns.\n\nAcross the lesson, the central conceptual relationship is that changing residual variance leads to heteroscedasticity, heteroscedasticity can reduce confidence in standard errors and inference, and economists use diagnostic and corrective approaches to support more reliable conclusions."
    },
    "study_tips": {
      "format": "markdown",
      "body": "## Study Tips\n\n- Focus on the spread of residuals, not the existence of residuals.\n- When analysing residual plots, look for changes in the width of the residual cloud.\n- Always justify conclusions using evidence from residual behaviour.\n- Distinguish between detection methods and remedies.\n- Remember that heteroscedasticity primarily affects the reliability of inference rather than automatically invalidating a regression model.\n- Complete the final evaluation activity before attempting the formative assessment to strengthen your judgement skills."
    }
