          "title": "Why Economists Care",
          "body_format": "markdown",
          "body": "## Practical Consequences\n\nHeteroscedasticity does not necessarily make coefficient estimates unusable. However, it can make standard errors unreliable. When standard errors are unreliable, confidence intervals and hypothesis tests may also become less reliable, affecting the conclusions researchers draw from data."
        },
        {
          "material_id": "A4-M2",
          "material_type": "modelling_note",
          "activity_id": "A4",
          "title": "How the Problem Affects Inference",
          "body_format": "markdown",
          "body": "Imagine that residual variance becomes larger for some observations and smaller for others. This means the constant-variance assumption is no longer holding consistently across the dataset. Because many standard error calculations depend on that assumption, the estimated standard errors may no longer reflect uncertainty accurately. When standard errors become unreliable, confidence intervals and hypothesis tests become less dependable as indicators of evidence. As a result, researchers may place too much or too little confidence in findings, making statistical inference less reliable overall."
        },
