          "material_id": "A5-M3",
          "material_type": "sample_output",
          "activity_id": "A5",
          "title": "Worked Judgement",
          "body_format": "markdown",
          "body": "In Scenario A, the residual plot already provides direct evidence of a widening pattern. Because the available evidence is visual and immediately observable, inspecting the residual plot is highly suitable. It is also simple to implement. However, visual inspection alone may not provide the same level of formal confirmation as a statistical test.\n\nA Breusch–Pagan test could add diagnostic depth, but it requires an additional analytical step. The trade-off is therefore simplicity versus stronger diagnostic evidence.\n\nIf the goal is to improve the reliability of inference after heteroscedasticity has been identified, robust standard errors become particularly valuable. They do not explain why variance changes, but they help address the consequences for statistical inference.\n\nConsidering the evidence available, the need for reliable inference, and practical simplicity, the most appropriate immediate action in Scenario A is to begin with the residual plot evidence and then use robust standard errors if inference is the main concern. This judgement places greater weight on the observed pattern while recognising the limitation that visual evidence alone may not fully diagnose the issue."
