          "body": "Heteroscedasticity occurs when residual variance changes across observations rather than remaining constant. Residual plots provide a practical way to detect changes in variability, while statistical tests such as the Breusch–Pagan test can provide supporting evidence. The main concern for economists is that changing variance can affect standard errors and therefore the reliability of statistical inference. Common responses include robust standard errors, transformations, and improved model design. When interpreting regression results, focus on evidence, patterns, and the implications for research conclusions."
